  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\n- Becoming Hot and Humid\n\nAs high pressure shifts east today, southwest flow behind the high\nwill advect warmer temperatures and higher dew points. Highs today around\n80 will increase into the mid 80s to low 90s by Tuesday. Dew\npoints will increase into the mid to upper 60s with the potential\nfor dew points in the low 70s on Tuesday and Wednesday. The\ncombination of these temperatures and dew points will result heat\nindex values in the 90s starting Monday. The hottest heat index\nvalues will be Tuesday and Wednesday with most of the area in the\nmid 90s. Areas toward south central Michigan may see heat index\nvalues approach 100 Tuesday.\n\n- Rain Chances Increase Tonight into Sunday\n\nThe low level jet causing showers and thunderstorms to develop in\neastern South Dakota and western Minnesota will gradually shift\neast today. A weak 500mb shortwave associated with this \nconvection will move in during the late afternoon and early \nevening paired with the 850mb low level jet convergence. \nInstability will be limited over the area therefore expected a \nweakening trend as it approaches western Michigan. Showers will \nexpand eastward overnight into Sunday as warm air advection and \nlow level convergence set up over the area. The thunder potential \nwill remain low into Sunday morning with the best chance for a \nrumble or two toward the lakeshore. Precipitable water values will\napproach 2 inches which is around the 90th percentile \nclimatologically for this time of year. There is the potential for\nsome pockets of heavier rainfall (around 2 inches) as seen in the\nlocalized probability matched mean of the HREF. The probability \nlooks higher toward the lakeshore as there will be a little more \ninstability to tap into (around 500 J/kg).\n\nMost of the rainfall is expected Sunday morning with a lull during\nthe afternoon. Areas toward US-10 could see rain all day. An\nadditional round of rain and thunderstorms is expected to develop\nSunday night into Monday morning with the low level jet \nconvergence and warm air advection.\n\n- Additional Chances for Rain and Thunderstorms Next Week\n\nForecast confidence decreases into next week as details get a\nlittle murky. Slight differences in the strength and placement of\nthe upper level ridge and shortwave troughs moving through will\nimpact our chances. With the mentioned high dew points and\nprecipitable water values, it won't take much to spark off showers\nand thunderstorms. Therefore there are chances each day, although\nthe best chances will be during the overnight timeframes matching\nwith the best low level jet convergence and warm air advection.",
